I think "better" is close to meaningless in that context.
It's important to learn fire, cast and melee so pick some
classes that do each and cyle.

> Questions I still have:
>
> * What are some usefull arrow management strategies and what are the
> plusses and minuses for characters up to about XL10?

When faced with popcorn monsters don't waste amunition
that might disappear or that you need to pick up.  Even at
XP=1 grid bugs are popcorn monsters.  As your character
advances the list of popcorn monsters gradually expends.

> ** Spam everything to get the skillups and switch to daggers until you
> can find more?

I tend to use only melee against popcorn monsters but
otherwise fire a lot to develop skills.  I end up advancing
dagger skill faster than bow skill because I never run out
of daggers whenever I survive a battle to be able to
retrieve them.

> ** Save arrows for tougher foes and Floating Eyes and skill up
> daggers?

Also, once expert in both dagger and arrow there's
some sense in spending other missiles on floating
eyes.  I've taken out a fair number of floating eyes
with rocks and I use rocks in Sokoban because I
don't mind burrying them under boulders.

> ** Balance arrows and daggers?

I don't know what numbers counts as balance, but
fire aggressively with both at anything not a popcorn
monster or at any crowd that isn't popcorn in its
aggregate  - At some point the largest group of orcs
or rothes or whatever become something you can just
wade through in melee looking for death drops; until
that fire away.


